{"text": " Because the price only matters to somebody who wants to sell, and you aren't selling. That bond could get cut in half, and nothing about your situation changes. The coupons will still show up on time, and the principal will still come back at maturity. So your pension problem has a very specific shape. You owe a fixed amount, on a fixed schedule, stretching decades out, which means whatever you buy to cover it needs that same shape. That rules out almost everything. You can't put Dwight's retirement in stocks. Stocks build wealth over time. Dwight needs $2,000 in cash on the first of the month guaranteed, and you can't take much risk with it because it isn't your money. You already promised it. If the market has a bad year, Dwight's check size doesn't get any smaller. So while everyone argues about term premiums, whether it's a good time to be buying, and whether the Fed chair sounded slightly more worried than he did last month, you're not even listening to these conversations, which makes you a very unique buyer. For 50 years, traditional pension funds were the best customers of the long end of the bond market, But they're the last of a dying breed. The secret force that held up the bond market for 50 years has now disappeared.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.9, "text": " Because the price only matters to somebody who wants to sell, and you aren't selling.", "tokens": [50363, 4362, 262, 2756, 691, 6067, 284, 8276, 508, 3382, 284, 3677, 11, 290, 345, 3588, 470, 6301, 13, 50583], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09469586565978545, "compression_ratio": 1.68, "no_speech_prob": 0.015164882875978947, "words": [{"word": " Because", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.18, "probability": 0.3453061282634735}, {"word": " the", "start": 0.18, "end": 0.36, "probability": 0.9811125993728638}, {"word": " price", "start": 0.36, "end": 0.64, "probability": 0.9969176054000854}, {"word": " only", "start": 0.64, "end": 0.88, "probability": 0.992394566535